---
// The discipline, briefly, and where each of its claims becomes a mechanism.
// The essay carries the long argument; this page carries the mapping, and
// every claim gets one figure that states it geometrically.
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import FigDemand from '../../components/figures/FigDemand.astro';
import FigSession from '../../components/figures/FigSession.astro';
import FigJudgment from '../../components/figures/FigJudgment.astro';
import FigDone from '../../components/figures/FigDone.astro';
import FigState from '../../components/figures/FigState.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const proof = `${base}/proof/presidio-input-side-pii-scrub`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Intent Engineering"
  eyebrow="The discipline"
  color="#cba6f7"
  intro="A layer has opened above the editor. Its input is raw intent, its output is code verified on the remote, and the discipline in between is deciding what deserves a person. This page is the short version and the mapping: five claims, and the mechanism where Continuum makes each one checkable."
>
  <div class="glass-pane p-6 md:p-8 mb-16">
    <p class="text-[0.9375rem] text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-[680px]">
      Writing code stopped being the scarce input. Well-formed intention became it. The
      work that decides an outcome now happens before an agent starts, turning demand into
      something executable, and after it stops, proving that what came back is what was
      asked for. Intent Engineering is the name for that work. Continuum is a workstation
      built for it, which means every claim below has to be a mechanism, not a belief.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-[0.75rem]">
      <a href="https://daviguides.github.io/articles/machine-learning/2026/04/25/intent-engineering.html"
         class="text-[#89b4fa] hover:text-text-primary">The founding essay, for the long argument →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-16">

    <!-- 1 -->
    <section class="movement">
      <div class="claim-head">
        <span class="claim-n">1</span>
        <h2>Demand arrives malformed.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="claim-body">
        A thread, a meeting, a feeling that a release is risky. None of it is executable.
        Turning it into ordered work is hours of unassisted thinking, and it is where the
        day actually goes.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-pane glass-pane">
          <FigDemand />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Refinement adds no material. It gives the same four things an order, and each one an acceptance criterion.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="claim-does">
        <strong>What Continuum does about it.</strong> A captured signal is explored into
        findings, findings are resolved into decisions, decisions are written out as briefs
        a competent stranger could execute: what is in scope, what is not, what done means,
        what it waits for.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-where">
        <a href={`${base}/docs/how-it-works/`}>the line, stage by stage →</a>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href={`${proof}/brief.md`}>a real brief →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 -->
    <section class="movement">
      <div class="claim-head">
        <span class="claim-n">2</span>
        <h2>The session is not the unit of work.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="claim-body">
        A context window fills and the understanding built inside it ends. Anything that
        treats the conversation as the container loses the work when the container closes.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-pane glass-pane">
          <FigSession />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The task crosses every break because each stage wrote down what the next one needs before it was allowed to open. The session is disposable by design; what it wrote down is not.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="claim-does">
        <strong>What Continuum does about it.</strong> The task is the unit. It moves
        through named stages, and each stage must leave a file on disk before the next may
        open. The next session resumes from files, not from memory.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-where">
        <a href={`${proof}/`}>what a task leaves behind →</a>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/how-it-works/`}>modes and gates →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 -->
    <section class="movement">
      <div class="claim-head">
        <span class="claim-n">3</span>
        <h2>Judgment does not delegate. It concentrates.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="claim-body">
        More agents do not need less supervision. They need supervision worth interrupting
        a person for. Everything else has to be decided without you, and be inspectable
        afterwards.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-pane glass-pane">
          <FigJudgment />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Three kinds of decision cross to a person. Every other call is made with its criterion recorded and a link that reopens the exact moment: supervision scales by spending less judgment, not by delegating it.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="claim-does">
        <strong>What Continuum does about it.</strong> A scope call, a fact only you know,
        an outward-facing effect: those stop and wait, and reach you as a message. The rest
        moves, and leaves a receipt you can disagree with after lunch.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-where">
        <a href={`${base}/#rails`}>the rails →</a>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/vocabulary/`}>receipts, defined →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 -->
    <section class="movement">
      <div class="claim-head">
        <span class="claim-n">4</span>
        <h2>Done is a claim until something checks it.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="claim-body">
        An agent that says it finished and an agent that finished look identical from the
        inside. Trust has to be replaced by a reading of something the agent does not
        control.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-pane glass-pane">
          <FigDone />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The two reports are identical; only one of them was measured against something the agent does not control. What could not be read reports as a gap, not as a pass.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="claim-does">
        <strong>What Continuum does about it.</strong> The verdict for a task comes from
        origin, never from the local checkout and never from the agent's own report. A
        task that publishes "done" against the evidence gets flagged instead of shipped.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-where">
        <a href={`${proof}/task.yaml`}>a real ledger →</a>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <a href={`${base}/docs/evidence/`}>the scoreboard →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 -->
    <section class="movement">
      <div class="claim-head">
        <span class="claim-n">5</span>
        <h2>Context is the artifact. Code is the byproduct.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="claim-body">
        If the reasoning lives in a vendor's database, the work dies with the vendor. If it
        lives beside the code, it outlives every session, branch, machine and model that
        touched it.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <div class="fig-pane glass-pane">
          <FigState />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Everything inside the box comes with a clone. Everything outside it can be replaced without the work losing anything.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="claim-does">
        <strong>What Continuum does about it.</strong> The path on disk is the structure:
        initiative, milestone, task. Anything that can read a directory can verify progress,
        including you, including a year from now.
      </p>
      <p class="claim-where">
        <a href={`${base}/docs/architecture/`}>three planes →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>

  <div class="glass-card p-6 mt-16">
    <span class="eyebrow block mb-3" style="color:#94e2d5;">Where it goes next</span>
    <p class="text-[0.875rem] text-text-muted max-w-[680px]">
      The discipline is older than the tooling: people have been doing this by hand, badly,
      since the first agent finished a task nobody checked. What Continuum adds is that
      each of the five claims above is something a directory can prove. Read
      <a href={`${base}/docs/how-it-works/`} class="text-text-secondary hover:text-text-primary">how the line runs</a>,
      or open <a href={`${proof}/`} class="text-text-secondary hover:text-text-primary">one real task</a> and
      check the claims against it.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  /* Figure token contract. The surface is dark in both themes, so ink
     inherits the body text colour and accent is the page's own gold. */
  .movement {
    --fig-ink: var(--color-text-muted, #a6adc8);
    --fig-accent: #d4af37;
    --fig-muted: var(--color-text-dim, #7f849c);
    --fig-label-font: 'Fira Code', ui-monospace, monospace;
  }

  .claim-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .claim-n {
    width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem; flex: none;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-surface1, #45475a); border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--color-text-dim, #7f849c);
  }
  .claim-head h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0; }

  .claim-body { font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary, #bac2de); max-width: 66ch; }

  .movement figure { margin: 1.75rem 0 1.25rem; }
  .fig-pane { padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem; }
  @media (min-width: 768px) { .fig-pane { padding: 2rem 1.75rem; } }
  .movement figure :global(svg.fig) { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .movement figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #a6adc8);
    max-width: 70ch; border-left: 2px solid rgba(212, 175, 55, 0.5); padding-left: 0.875rem;
  }

  .claim-does { font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-text-muted, #a6adc8); max-width: 70ch; }
  .claim-does strong { color: var(--color-text-primary, #cdd6f4); font-weight: 600; }

  .claim-where { margin-top: 0.625rem; font-family: 'Fira Code', monospace; font-size: 0.6875rem; }
  .claim-where a { color: var(--color-text-dim, #7f849c); }
  .claim-where a:hover { color: var(--color-text-primary, #cdd6f4); }
  .claim-where .sep { color: var(--color-surface1, #45475a); margin: 0 0.5rem; }
</style>
